{
  "id": "NP03Z",
  "version": "2.0",
  "title": "SNS device insertion",
  "description": "Sacral neuromodulation (SNS) is a treatment where a small device is implanted near your sacral nerves to send electrical pulses that help control bladder, bowel, or pain issues. The procedure involves placing an electrode near these nerves and connecting it to a pulse generator (battery pack) under your skin. Before permanent implantation, you may have a trial period with temporary leads to confirm the treatment works for you. The outpatient implantation typically takes 1-2 hours under general anesthesia. You can return home the same day with careful activity restrictions while healing. The device can be adjusted or removed if needed, and battery replacement is required every few years. This type of procedure can be performed various ways, including revision/removal, implantation, electrode array, and implantation, pulse generator / definitive, and the cost may vary depending on the specific approach or service provided.",
